1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,335
In 1901, sponge divers pulled a lump of corroded bronze from a Roman-era shipwreck.

2
00:00:06,835 --> 00:00:08,437
Inside it were gears.

3
00:00:08,987 --> 00:00:13,898
At least 30 of them, cut by hand, more than 2,000 years ago.

4
00:00:14,348 --> 00:00:16,377
This is the Antikythera Mechanism.

5
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:20,524
Turn a handle, and it showed the Sun, the Moon, and the planets,

6
00:00:20,724 --> 00:00:25,256
predicted eclipses, and even tracked the dates of the Olympic Games.

7
00:00:25,756 --> 00:00:29,708
Only about a third of it survived, in 82 fragments.

8
00:00:30,128 --> 00:00:36,706
In 2021, a team at University College London rebuilt its front display from X-ray scans.

9
00:00:37,156 --> 00:00:41,647
Nothing this complex appears again for more than 1,000 years.

10
00:00:42,247 --> 00:00:46,102
So was it one of a kind, or the only one that survived?
